            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{v}:x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{h}:y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}:x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\nHence a **non‑sunny** line must be parallel to exactly one of \\(\\ell_{v},\\ell_{h},\\ell_{-1}\\).\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration of three distinct lines exists with exactly two sunny lines.  \nLet \\(L\\) be the unique non‑sunny line; the other two lines are sunny.  \nWe treat the three possible orientations of \\(L\\) separately, and for each orientation we consider every constant that allows \\(L\\) to contain points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(L\\) vertical \\((x=c)\\)\n\nThe only admissible constants are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(L:x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:  \n  \\[\n  S_{1}=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n  The three pairwise slopes are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(2,1)-(2,2):\\text{vertical }(\\parallel\\ell_{v}),\\\\\n  &(2,1)-(3,1):\\text{horizontal }(\\parallel\\ell_{h}),\\\\\n  &(2,2)-(3,1):\\frac{1-2}{3-2}=-1\\;(\\parallel\\ell_{-1}).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  Hence any line through two points of \\(S_{1}\\) is non‑sunny.  \n  Each sunny line can therefore contain **at most one** point of \\(S_{1}\\); with only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points of \\(S_{1}\\), leaving one uncovered – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L:x=2\\) contains \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The only pair among \\(S_{2}\\) whose line is not parallel to \\(\\ell_{v},\\ell_{h},\\ell_{-1}\\) is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n  Thus a sunny line can cover at most the two points \\((1,2),(3,1)\\); the remaining three points \\((1,1),(1,3)\\) each require a separate sunny line, but only one sunny line remains. Contradiction.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  \\(L:x=3\\) contains only \\((3,1)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Among these, the sunny pairs are \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)) and \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n  Any other pair is vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\).  \n  Consequently the two sunny lines can cover at most the four points of the two sunny pairs, leaving \\((1,2)\\) uncovered. Contradiction.\n\nThus a vertical non‑sunny line cannot yield a valid configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(L\\) horizontal \\((y=c)\\)\n\nPossible constants are \\(c=1,2,3\\).\n\n* **\\(c=1\\).**  \n  \\(L:y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{4}=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Their pairwise slopes are vertical, horizontal, and \\(-1\\); therefore any line through two of them is non‑sunny.  \n  Each sunny line can contain at most one of these three points, but we have only two sunny lines – impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  \\(L:y=2\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Uncovered points:  \n\n  \\[\n  S_{5}=\\{(1,1)