issible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\; \\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor \\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb N^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\operatorname{lev}(L)\\) the set of \u201clevels\u2019\u2019  \n\\(L\\cap L_s\\) with \\(L_s:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nA *sunny* line is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\[\n\\mathcal D:=\\{\\,\\text{horizontal},\\;\\text{vertical},\\;x+y=0\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L\\) is sunny, then for each level \\(L_s\\) the intersection \\(L\\cap L_s\\) contains **at most one** point; otherwise two distinct points of the same level would determine a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.  \nHence a sunny line meets at most one point of each level, i.e.\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAssume that among the \\(n\\) required lines exactly \\(k\\) are sunny and the remaining \\(n-k\\) are non\u2011sunny (hence parallel to a member of \\(\\mathcal D\\)).  \nLet \\(S\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) be the set of levels that are covered by at least one non\u2011sunny line.  \nEvery non\u2011sunny line is parallel to a level, therefore it covers **all** points of the level it is parallel to. Consequently the total number of points covered by the non\u2011sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll remaining points must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which can contribute at most one point per level, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points in total. Using (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;-\\;\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)\\;\\le\\; kn . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S|=n-k\\) (there are exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines), the left\u2011hand side of (7) is maximal when the levels in \\(S\\) are the largest possible ones, namely  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\,n+1,n,\\dots ,n+2-(n-k)\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor this choice  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s\\in S}(s-1)= (n-k)n-\\frac{(n-k)(n-k-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into (7) and simplifying yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy (8); in particular at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are forced to be non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sufficient construction for every \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad\\bigl(m\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  Covering the upper part of the triangle\n\nConsider the \\(m\\) largest levels  \n\n\\[\nL_{n+1},L_{n},\\dots ,L_{n+2-m}.\n\\]\n\nFor each of them we take the line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) that coincides with the level:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=n+1,n,\\dots ,n+2-m)\\,}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEvery line in (9) is non\u2011sunny, and together they cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum \\(a+b\\) is at least \\(n+2-m\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The remaining lower triangle\n\nAfter removing the points covered in 2.1 we are left with  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\na triangular lattice of size \\(k\\).  \nWe now exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover \\(T_k\\).\n\nFor each \\(r\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\lambda_r:\\; y=\\frac{r}{k+1-r}\\,x+\\frac{r}{k+1-r}\\,}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\lambda_r\\) passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_r=(r,1),\\qquad Q_r=(k+1-r,k+1-r),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(T_k\\).  \nBecause the slope \\(\\dfrac{r}{k+1-r}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1