e \\(\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and contains \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the vertical lines (if its first coordinate is \\(\\le n-1\\)) or on \\(\\ell\\) (the point \\((n,1)\\)). Thus \\(k=1\\) is also always attainable.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Assume, to the contrary, that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   Let  \n\n   * \\(V\\) be the set of vertical non‑sunny lines,\n   * \\(H\\) the set of horizontal non‑sunny lines,\n   * \\(D\\) the set of diagonal non‑sunny lines (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n   Denote \\(|V|=v,\\;|H|=h,\\;|D|=d\\).  \n   Because there are \\(n\\) lines in total and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\n\n   \\[\n   v+h+d=n-k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\n   \\]\n\n   be the *\\(s\\)-diagonal*.  \\(|\\Delta_s|=s-1\\).\n\n   *If the diagonal \\(\\Delta_s\\) belongs to \\(D\\), all its points are already covered.*  \n   *Otherwise* a point \\((a,s-a)\\in\\Delta_s\\) can be covered only by a vertical line \\(x=a\\in V\\) or by a horizontal line \\(y=s-a\\in H\\).  \n   Consequently, when \\(\\Delta_s\\notin D\\) the number of points of \\(\\Delta_s\\) that are covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   c_s\\le v+h. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (1) we have \\(v+h=n-k-d\\le n-2-d\\).  \n\n   Since there are only \\(d\\) diagonals in \\(D\\), at least \\(n-d\\) diagonals are **not** in \\(D\\).  \n   Choose among those a diagonal \\(\\Delta_{s_0}\\) with the smallest possible index \\(s_0\\).  \n   As there are at least two diagonals outside \\(D\\) (because \\(d\\le n-2\\)), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   s_0\\le n-1. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   From (2) and (3),\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{covered points of }\\Delta_{s_0}\\le v+h\\le n-2-d\\le (s_0-1)-2,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. at most \\((s_0-3)\\) points of \\(\\Delta_{s_0}\\) are covered 