can cover at most one point of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\).  \n   Consequently the total number of points of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) that can be covered is at most\n   \\[\n   v\\;(\\text{already covered})\\;+\\;(h+s)\\;(\\text{one per non\u2011vertical line})=v+h+s=n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   However \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) points.  \n   Equality in (5) would require that **every** non\u2011vertical line actually meets \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) (so that each contributes one new point) and that all \\(v\\) vertical lines meet \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) (which they do).  \n   This forces the following:\n\n   * For each column \\(a\\) whose assigned line is horizontal, the horizontal line must pass through the point \\(Q_a\\).  \n     But a horizontal line has equation \\(y=c\\); it passes through \\(Q_a=(a,n-a)\\) only when \\(c=n-a\\).  \n     The same value of \\(c\\) would then be forced for every horizontal column, which is impossible because distinct horizontal lines must have distinct constants.  \n     Hence **no column can be assigned a horizontal line**: \\(h=0\\).\n\n   With \\(h=0\\) we have from (2) \\(v+s=n\\) and \\(s=k\\).  \n   The set \\(U\\) now consists exactly of the \\(k\\) columns whose assigned line is sunny.  \n   Each sunny line meets \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) in at most one point, and the \\(v\\) vertical lines meet \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) in the \\(v\\) points \\(Q_a\\) with vertical columns.  \n   Therefore the total number of points of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) covered is at most \\(v+k=n\\).  \n   Since \\(|\\Delta_{n}|=n-1\\), we must have \\(v+k\\ge n-1\\), i.e.\n   \\[\n   (n-k)+k\\ge n-1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;n\\ge n-1,\n   \\]\n   which is always true, but we need **strict** equality to avoid an uncovered point.  \n   Equality would require that the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover **all** the remaining \\(k\\) points of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\).  \n   However, the \\(k\\) points of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) that are not already covered by vertical lines are precisely\n   \\[\n   Q_{a}= (a,n-a)\\qquad\\text{for the }k\\text{ columns }a\\text{ whose line is sunny}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   By construction each such sunny line passes through the corresponding point \\(P_a\\) of \\(\\Delta_{n+1}\\); its slope is different from \\(-1\\).  \n   Two distinct sunny lines cannot both pass through the same point of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) (otherwise they would coincide, contradicting distinctness).  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\), and they **do** cover exactly those \\(k\\) points listed in (6).  \n\n   The problem now is that the point \\(Q_{n}= (n,0)\\) does **not** belong to \\(T_n\\); the diagonal \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) stops at \\(a=n-1\\).  \n   Consequently the set of points of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\) that remain uncovered is\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_{n}\\setminus\\bigl(\\{Q_a\\mid a\\text{ vertical}\\}\\cup\\{Q_a\\mid a\\text{ sunny}\\}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n   Because \\(h=0\\) we have exactly \\(v=n-k\\) vertical columns, so the uncovered points are those \\(Q_a\\) with \\(a\\) belonging to the **sunny** columns **except** the one that also belongs to a vertical column.  \n   But a column cannot be both vertical and sunny, so the uncovered set is empty only when **every** column is either vertical or sunny, i.e. when \\(v+h+s=n\\) with \\(h=0\\).  \n   This forces \\(v=n-k\\) and \\(s=k\\).  \n\n   Now look at the point \\((1,n)\\), which lies on \\(\\Delta_{n+1}\\) with column \\(a=1\\).  \n   If the line assigned to column \\(1\\) is sunny, then that sunny line already contains the point \\((1,n)\\) and, because its slope is not \\(-1\\), it cannot also contain any other point of \\(\\Delta_{n+1}\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-1\\) points of \\(\\Delta_{n+1}\\) must therefore be covered by the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines.  \n   The vertical line \\(x=1\\) would be needed, but column \\(1\\) is already occupied by a sunny line, so the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is unavailable.  \n   Hence the point \\((1,n)\\) would be uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n   The same argument works for any sunny column: the point of \\(\\Delta_{n+1}\\) belonging to that column cannot be covered by a vertical line, and the only line that can cover it is the sunny line itself.  \n   Consequently **each** sunny line forces the loss of the vertical line of the same column, and the point of \\(\\Delta_{n+1}\\) belonging to that column would then be covered only by the sunny line.  \n   Since we have at least two sunny lines, at least two points of \\(\\Delta_{n+1}\\) are covered by sunny lines, and the remaining \\(n-2\\) points must be covered by vertical lines.  \n   But we have only \\(n-k\\le n-2\\) vertical lines, exactly enough in number; however the vertical lines must be placed at the **remaining** columns, i.e. at the columns that are not sunny.  \n   This is possible, but now consider diagonal \\(\\Delta_{n}\\).  \n   The two sunny columns, say \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), contribute the points \\(Q_a\\) and \\(Q_b\\) to \\(\\Delta_{n}\\).  \n   Those two points are already covered (by the sunny lines).  \n   The vertical lines cover the other \\(n-2\\) points of \\(\\Delta_{n}\\).  \n   The problem appears when we look at the diagonal \\(\\Delta_{n-1}\\), which consists of the points \\((a,n-1-a)\\) for \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n   For a sunny column \\(a\\) the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is missing, and the only line that can meet \\(\\Delta_{n-1}\\) at the point \\((a,n-1-a)\\) is the sunny line through \\((a,n+1-a)\\).  \n   That sunny line does **not** pass through \\((a,n-1-a)\\) because the two points have different second coordinates; a line cannot contain both unless it is vertical, which it is not.  \n   Hence the point \\((a,n-1-a)\\) of \\(\\Delta_{n-1}\\) is uncovered.  \n   Because we have at least two sunny columns, at least two points of \\(\\Delta_{n-1}\\) remain uncovered, contradicting the covering requirement.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable; the impossibility argument shows that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}}\n\\]"
