"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nAll non\u2011negative integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\nare attainable, and no other values are possible (obviously \\(k\\le n\\) because there are only \\(n\\) lines).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n*Notation.*  \n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).  \nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n*Idea.*  \nWe cover the points of the leftmost \\(n-k\\) columns by the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining columns are  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(n-k+j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\},\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus column \\(C_j\\) contains exactly \\(k+1-j\\) points; the whole set of points that are still uncovered is the right\u2011hand triangular set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}C_j ,\n\\qquad |\\mathcal T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe shall show that **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) admits a family of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover every point of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  \nTogether with the vertical lines (1) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Construction of the sunny lines.*  \nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(n-k+j,\\;j) \\in C_j .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\) are distinct.  \nFor each \\(j\\) we draw the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;y-j=\\frac{1-j}{k-j}\\bigl(x-(n-k+j)\\bigr) .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The fraction \\(\\dfrac{1-j}{k-j}\\) is well defined because \\(j\\neq k\\); for \\(j=k\\) we simply take the vertical line \\(x=n\\), which is **not** sunny, and replace it by the line through \\(P_k\\) and the point \\((n,2)\\); this change only affects the case \\(j=k\\) and guarantees that every \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.)\n\n*Why each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.*  \nThe slope in (5) equals \\(\\dfrac{1-j}{k-j}\\).  \nIt can never be \\(0\\) (numerator \\(1-j\\neq0\\) for \\(j\\ge1\\)), never \\(\\infty\\) (denominator \\(k-j\\neq0\\) for \\(j\\le k-1\\)), and it equals \\(-1\\) only when  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-j}{k-j}=-1\\Longleftrightarrow 1-j=-(k-j)\\Longleftrightarrow 2j=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd the unique index \\(j_0=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) would give slope \\(-1\\); for this index we replace the second point \\((n,1)\\) in the definition of \\(\\ell_{j_0}\\) by \\((n,2)\\).  The new slope is then \\(\\dfrac{2-j_0}{k-j_0}\\neq-1\\).  Hence every \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny and the \\(\\ell_j\\)\u2019s are pairwise distinct because they have different slopes (or, for the modified line, a different intercept).\n\n*Coverage of the triangular set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).*  \nFix a column \\(C_j\\) (so the abscissa is \\(x=n-k+j\\)).  \nFor any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb=j+(b-j)=j+\\frac{1-j}{k-j}\\bigl((n-k+j)-(n-k+j)\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nand therefore the point \\((n-k+j,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_{b}\\) (the line whose index equals the ordinate \\(b\\)).  \nConsequently **every** point of \\(C_j\\) belongs to exactly one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n*Putting everything together.*  \nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and they cover\n\n* all points of the leftmost \\(n-k\\) columns (by the vertical lines), and  \n* all points of the right\u2011hand triangle \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) (by the sunny lines).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.  This construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Impossibility