            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are called **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach line in (3) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and hence lies on exactly one of the lines (3).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand one sunny line that passes through the unique point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(n\\), namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nFor instance  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=\\frac12(x-n)+1\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(\\frac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and contains \\((n,1)\\).  \nThe vertical lines (4) cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) is covered by \\(\\ell\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The vertical (non‑sunny) lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical, hence non‑sunny, and they cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The right‑hand triangular set that remains uncovered\n\nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) put  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(n-k+j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_k\\) are pairwise disjoint, their union is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k:=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}C_j,\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nand \\(|\\mathcal T_k|=\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) that are not already covered by the vertical lines (6) belong to \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the point  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(n-k+j,\\;j)\\in C_j .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nNow we construct \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even* (\\(k=2m\\)).  \nFor every \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;y-j=\\frac{1-j}{k-j}\\bigl(x-(n-k+j)\\bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd* (\\(k=2m+1\\)).  \nFor all \\(j\\neq\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) we use the same formula (10).  \nFor the special index  \n\n\\[\nj_0:=\\frac{k+1}{2}\n\\]\n\nwe replace the second point \\((n,1)\\) that appears in (10) by \\((n,2)\\); equivalently we define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{j_0}:\\;y-j_0=\\frac{2-j_0}{k-j_0}\\bigl(x-(n-k+j_0)\\bigr).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Verification that the \\(\\ell_j\\) are sunny and distinct\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_j\\) given by (10) equals \\(\\displaystyle m_j=\\frac{1-j}{k-j}\\).\n\n* \\(m_j\\neq0\\) because \\(1-j\\neq0\\) for every \\(j\\ge1\\);\n* \\(m_j\\neq\\infty\\) because \\(k-j\\neq0\\) for every \\(j\\le k-1\\);\n* \\(m_j=-1\\) would require \\(1-j=-(k-j)\\), i.e. \\(2j=k+1\\).  \n  When \\(k\\) is even this never happens; when \\(k\\) is odd the unique index \\(j_0\\) with \\(2j_0=k+1\\) is treated by (11), whose slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{2-j_0}{k-j_0}\\neq-1\\).\n\nHence every \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.  \nTwo different indices give different slopes (or, for the modified line, a different intercept), so the lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Coverage of the set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)\n\nFix a column \\(C_j\\) (so the abscissa is \\(x=n-k+j\\)).  \nTake any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\).  \nConsider the line \\(\\ell_b\\).  By construction \\(\\ell_b\\) passes through the point \\(P_b=(n-k+b,b)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell_b\\) has slope \\(m_b\\) and contains \\(P_b\\), its equation can be written as  \n\n\\[\ny-b=m_b\\bigl(x-(n-k+b)\\bigr).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=n-k+j\\) (the abscissa of the column) we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny-b=m_b\\bigl(j-b\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m_b=\\dfrac{1-b}{k-b}\\), the right–hand side simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-b}{k-b}\\,(j-b)=\\frac{(1-b)(j-b)}{k-b}=j-b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(y-b=j-b\\) and consequently \\(y=j\\).  Hence the point \\((n-k+j,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \n\nBecause \\(b\\) was an arbitrary element of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k+1-j\\}\\), **every** point of the column \\(C_j\\) belongs to the sunny line whose index equals its second coordinate.  Therefore the family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6  Completion of the covering\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\bigr\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by the discussion in §§2.1 and 2.5 they together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).  Hence for the chosen \\(k\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nThe problem asks for **exactly** \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  No further restriction exists, because the construction of §2 works for every \\(k\\) in this range.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Answer\n\nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable, and no other values are possible.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
