                "content": "**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, every non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) is possible **except** \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); all other lines are *sunny*.\n\n2. **The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible.**  \n   Using \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines we may, without loss of generality, take the vertical lines  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n   \\]\n   These cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   A=(n-1,2),\\qquad B=(n-1,1),\\qquad C=(n,1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains two of the points in (1) is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); consequently a *sunny* line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \n   Hence at least three sunny lines are needed to cover \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\), contradicting the requirement that exactly two sunny lines be used.  \n   Therefore **\\(k=2\\) cannot occur for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   All of them are non‑sunny and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny) together with any sunny line through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Covering the right‑hand triangular remainder with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\ge 3\\)).**  \n\n   After the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\{(n-k+j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\Bigr\\},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   a right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\) containing \\(\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n   For each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(n-k+j,\\;j)\\in\\mathcal T_k .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\\ell_j:\\;y-j=\\frac{j-1}{j-k}\\bigl(x-(n-k+j)\\bigr)\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k)\\;}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   (the denominator never vanishes because \\(j\\neq k\\)).  \n   The slope of \\(\\ell_j\\) equals \\(\\displaystyle m_j=\\frac{j-1}{j-k}\\).  \n   *\\(m_j\\neq0\\)* because \\(j-1\\neq0\\) for \\(j\\ge2\\) and for \\(j=1\\) we have \\(m_1=0\\) **only when \\(k=1\\)**, a case already treated; here \\(k\\ge3\\) so \\(m_1\\neq0\\).  \n   *\\(m_j\\neq\\infty\\)* because the denominator \\(j-k\\neq0\\).  \n   *\\(m_j\\neq-1\\)* would require \\(j-1=-(j-k)\\), i.e. \\(2j=k+1\\). Since \\(k\\ge3\\) this equation has at most one integer solution; if it occurs (which happens only when \\(k\\) is odd) we replace the line \\(\\ell_{(k+1)/2}\\) by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{(k+1)/2}:\\;y-\\frac{k+1}{2}= \\frac{2-\\frac{k+1}{2}}{\\frac{k+1}{2}-k}\\bigl(x-(n-\\frac{k-1}{2})\\bigr),\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   whose slope is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{2-\\frac{k+1}{2}}{\\frac{k+1}{2}-k}\\neq-1\\).  \n   Consequently **every \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny** and the \\(\\ell_j\\) are pairwise distinct because they have distinct slopes (or, for the modified line, a distinct intercept).\n\n   **Incidence.**  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\). Then \\(x=n-k+j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and \\(1\\le y\\le k+1-j\\).  \n   Set \\(b:=y\\). By construction the line \\(\\ell_b\\) passes through the point \\(P_b=(n-k+b,b)\\). Substituting \\(x=n-k+j\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_b\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y-b=\\frac{b-1}{b-k}\\bigl((n-k+j)-(n-k+b)\\bigr)\n        =\\frac{b-1}{b-k}(j-b)=j-b .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(y=j\\). Because \\(j\\) is exactly the abscissa index of the column that contains \\((x,y)\\), we obtain \\(y=b\\). Thus \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \n   Consequently **every point of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) belongs to one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\)**.\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and by the discussion in §§4.1–4.5 it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n6. **Impossibility of larger \\(k\\).**  \n   Because we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, we can never have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines; thus \\(k>n\\) is impossible.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe only excluded value is \\(k=2\\).  \n\n---  \n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are called *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\nReplace every non‑sunny line by a vertical line without decreasing the\nset of covered points: any horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can be replaced by the\nvertical line \\(x=1\\) (which already belongs to the set of points), and any\ndiagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can be replaced by the vertical line \\(x=c-1\\).\nThus we may suppose that the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are the vertical\nlines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) except  \n\n\\[\nA=(n-1,2),\\qquad B=(n-1,1),\\qquad C=(n,1) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAny line containing two of the points in (4) is either vertical,\nhorizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at\nmost one of them.  Hence at least three sunny lines are needed to cover\n\\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\), contradicting the hypothesis that there are only two.\nTherefore **\\(k=2\\) cannot occur for any \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny, and each \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence it lies on exactly one of the lines\n(5).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ntogether with any sunny line through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\); for\nexample  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The right‑hand triangular remainder\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\nAfter the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nthe uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_k=\n\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\{(n-k+j,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le k+1-j\\Bigr\\},\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\na right‑angled triangle of side‑length \\(k\\) containing\n\\(\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  A family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal T_k\\)\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) put  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(n-k+j,\\;j)\\in\\mathcal T_k .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;y-j=\\frac{j-1}{j-k}\\bigl(x-(n-k+j)\\bigr)\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_j\\) is \\(m_j=\\dfrac{j-1}{j-k}\\).\n\n*The lines are sunny.*  \n\\(m_j\\neq0\\) because \\(j-1\\neq0\\) for every \\(j\\ge2\\) and for \\(j=1\\) we have\n\\(m_1=\\dfrac{0}{1-k}=0\\) only when \\(k=1\\); here \\(k\\ge3\\), so \\(m_1\\neq0\\).\n\\(m_j\\neq\\infty\\) because the denominator \\(j-k\\neq0\\).\nIf \\(m_j=-1\\) then \\(j-1=-(j-k)\\), i.e. \\(2j=k+1\\).  This can happen at\nmost once (when \\(k\\) is odd).  In that single case we replace \\(\\ell_j\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;y-j=\\frac{2-j}{j-k}\\bigl(x-(n-k+j)\\bigr),\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nwhose slope is \\(\\dfrac{2-j}{j-k}\\neq-1\\).  Hence every \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny,\nand the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n*Incidence.*  \nLet \\((x,y)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\).  Then there is a unique index\n\\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) with \\(x=n-k+j\\) and \\(1\\le y\\le k+1-j\\).\nSet \\(b:=y\\).  The line \\(\\ell_b\\) passes through the point\n\\(P_b=(n-k+b,b)\\).  Substituting \\(x=n-k+j\\) into the equation of\n\\(\\ell_b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny-b=\\frac{b-1}{b-k}\\bigl((n-k+j)-(n-k+b)\\bigr)\n      =\\frac{b-1}{b-k}(j-b)=j-b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(y=j\\).  Because \\(j\\) is precisely the column index of \\((x,y)\\),\nwe obtain \\(y=b\\); consequently \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).\nHence **every point of \\(\\mathcal T_k\\) belongs to one of the lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Completion of the covering\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nand by §§2.4–2.5 it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  Therefore for every\n\\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nBecause the problem demands exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, we can never\nhave more than \\(n\\) sunny lines; thus \\(k>n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Answer\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe value \\(k=2\\) is the unique exception, as proved in §1."
