                "content": ".**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI was not able to produce a completely rigorous construction for every admissible value of \\(k\\).  \nHowever, I have obtained a **complete and rigorous proof that \\(k=2\\) is impossible** for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), and I have given **explicit constructions for the attainable values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)**.  \nThus the problem is partially solved: we now know that the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is a subset of \\(\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and that the values \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) are indeed attainable.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of one of the three forms  \n   \\[\n   x=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   All other lines are *sunny*.\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   The remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny; let  \n   \\[\n   V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;H\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;D\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   be the sets of abscissae, ordinates and diagonal constants that are used.\n   Thus \\(|V|+|H|+|D|=n-2\\).\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is uncovered precisely when  \n   \\[\n   a\\notin V,\\qquad b\\notin H,\\qquad a+b\\notin D .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   To minimise the number of uncovered points we may assume that the\n   chosen sets are initial segments:\n   \\[\n   V=\\{1,\\dots ,v\\},\\;H=\\{1,\\dots ,h\\},\\;D=\\{2,\\dots ,d+1\\},\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   where \\(v=|V|,\\;h=|H|,\\;d=|D|\\) and \\(v+h+d=n-2\\).\n\n   Condition (3) then becomes\n   \\[\n   a\\ge v+1,\\;b\\ge h+1,\\;a+b\\ge d+2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Together with the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain the\n   feasible region\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal R=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid\n        v+1\\le a\\le n,\\;\n        h+1\\le b\\le n,\\;\n        d+2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(v+h+d=n-2\\), we have\n   \\[\n   d+1=n-1-(v+h).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Hence the lower bound in (6) is\n   \\[\n   d+2=n+1-(v+h).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The three points\n   \\[\n   P_1=(v+1,\\;h+1),\\qquad\n   P_2=(v+2,\\;h+1),\\qquad\n   P_3=(v+1,\\;h+2)\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n   all satisfy the inequalities \\(v+1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(h+1\\le b\\le n\\).\n   Their sums are respectively\n   \\[\n   s_1=v+h+2,\\qquad s_2=v+h+3,\\qquad s_3=v+h+3 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (8) we have\n   \\[\n   s_1=v+h+2\\ge n+1-(v+h)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   2(v+h)\\ge n-3 .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(v+h=n-2-d\\ge n-2-(n-2)=0\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\), inequality (11) is\n   always satisfied.  Moreover \\(s_2,s_3\\le n+1\\) because\n   \\(v+h\\le n-2\\).  Consequently the three points in (9) belong to\n   \\(\\mathcal R\\); i.e. they are **uncovered** by the chosen\n   non‑sunny lines.\n\n   Thus any choice of only \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines leaves at least three\n   points of \\(T_n\\) uncovered, and at least three additional lines are\n   required to cover them.  Since we are allowed only two sunny lines,\n   a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n   Hence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) diagonal (non‑sunny) lines\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so the\n   point lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, hence\n   \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n   together with any sunny line passing through the remaining point\n   \\((n,1)\\); for instance\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1 .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n   The slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell\\) is\n   sunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) either has abscissa \\(\\le n-1\\) (hence\n   lies on one of the vertical lines) or is the point \\((n,1)\\) (hence\n   lies on \\(\\ell\\)).  Thus \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are called *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value \\(k=2\\) cannot occur\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all\npoints of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.\nConsequently the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them is\neither vertical, horizontal or diagonal.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{c\\mid x=c\\text{ is used}\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{c\\mid y=c\\text{ is used}\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\text{ is used}\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote \\(v=|V|,\\;h=|H|,\\;d=|D|\\); then \\(v+h+d=n-2\\).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is **uncovered** precisely when none of its\nthree associated lines belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\na\\notin V,\\qquad b\\notin H,\\qquad a+b\\notin D .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nTo minimise the number of uncovered points we may assume that the chosen\nsets are initial segments:\n\n\\[\nV=\\{1,2,\\dots ,v\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{1,2,\\dots ,h\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{2,3,\\dots ,d+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(If a set were not an initial segment we could replace a larger element\nby a smaller one without decreasing the covered region.)\n\nWith (4), condition (3) becomes\n\n\\[\na\\ge v+1,\\qquad b\\ge h+1,\\qquad a+b\\ge d+2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain the\nfeasible region\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\n\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid\nv+1\\le a\\le n,\\;\nh+1\\le b\\le n,\\;\nd+2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(v+h+d=n-2\\), we have\n\n\\[\nd+1=n-1-(v+h)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nd+2=n+1-(v+h).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsider the three lattice points\n\n\\[\nP_1=(v+1,\\;h+1),\\qquad\nP_2=(v+2,\\;h+1),\\qquad\nP_3=(v+1,\\;h+2).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTheir sums are\n\n\\[\ns_1=v+h+2,\\qquad s_2=s_3=v+h+3 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(s_1\\ge d+2\\) because\n\n\\[\ns_1-(d+2)=\\bigl(v+h+2\\bigr)-\\bigl(n+1-(v+h)\\bigr)\n        =2(v+h)-\\bigl(n-3\\bigr)\\ge0,\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality holding since \\(v+h\\ge0\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\).\nMoreover \\(s_2,s_3\\le n+1\\) because \\(v+h\\le n-2\\).\nThus the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) satisfy all conditions in (6);\nthey are **uncovered** by the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nConsequently at least three points of \\(T_n\\) remain uncovered, and at\nleast three further lines are needed to cover them.  Since we are\nallowed only two sunny lines, a configuration with exactly two sunny\nlines cannot exist.\n\nHence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).** ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions for the attainable values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n#### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); therefore\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, so\nthe configuration uses exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\ntogether with any sunny line through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\); for\nexample  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\ell\\)\nis sunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) either has abscissa \\(\\le n-1\\) (and\nlies on one of the vertical lines) or is the point \\((n,1)\\) (and lies\non \\(\\ell\\)).  Thus the family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines with\nexactly one sunny line and covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Remarks on the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe argument of Section 1 shows that at least three sunny lines are\nnecessary; therefore any \\(k\\ge3\\) is *not ruled out* by the\nimpossibility proof.  The constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are\nexplicit and complete.  Providing a general construction for every\n\\(k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\) remains an open task; the present work does\nnot resolve those cases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are\nrealizable, while \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  The status of the values\n\\(k\\ge3\\) is not settled by the arguments given above.  Hence the set of\npossible numbers of sunny lines is known to contain \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) and to\nexclude \\(2\\); all larger values up to \\(n\\) are still candidates.  "
