ered** by the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.  Consequently at least\n   three points of \\(T_n\\) remain uncovered, but only two sunny lines are\n   available – a contradiction.  Thus\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\neq 2\\text{ for any }n\\ge3}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so the\n   point lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, giving a\n   configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1 . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence\n   \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) either has abscissa\n   \\(\\le n-1\\) (and lies on one of the vertical lines) or is the point\n   \\((n,1)\\) (and lies on \\(\\ell\\)).  Thus we obtain a configuration with\n   exactly one sunny line.\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   *Step 5.1 – A family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points with\n   \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y = -\\,i\\,x + i(k+2),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k . \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(-i\\) is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is\n   sunny and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(a\\le k\\).  Substituting\n   \\(b=k+2-a\\) into the equation of \\(L_a\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b = -a\\cdot a + a(k+2)=k+2-a ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  Consequently **every**\n   lattice point of \\(T_n\\) whose sum does not exceed \\(k+1\\) belongs to\n   one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   *Step 5.2 – Covering the remaining points.*  \n   The points of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(s\\ge k+2\\) are exactly the points of the\n   diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=s,\\qquad s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-(k+1)=n-k\\) such diagonals.  Use the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=s\\qquad(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1). \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Each of these lines is non‑sunny, they are distinct, and together with\n   the \\(k\\) sunny lines from (12) we have exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct\n   lines.\n\n   *Step 5.3 – Verification.*  \n   – If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), then by Step 5.1 it lies on\n   \\(L_a\\).  \n   – If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\), then it lies on the diagonal line\n   \\(x+y=a+b\\) which is one of the lines listed in (14).  \n\n   Hence **all** points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.  The construction uses\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, so it works for\n   every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   From Sections 2–5 we have:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable (explicit constructions).  \n   * \\(k=2\\) is impossible (Section 2).  \n   * For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists\n     (Section 5).\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are called *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all\npoints of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nConsequently the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them is\nvertical, horizontal or diagonal.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{c\\mid y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\nand denote  \n\n\\[\nv=|V|,\\;h=|H|,\\;d=|D|,\\qquad v+h+d=n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is **uncovered** precisely when none of its\nthree associated lines belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\na\\notin V,\\qquad b\\notin H,\\qquad a+b\\notin D . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nReplacing each of the three sets by the initial segment of the same\ncardinality can only increase the set of uncovered points: if, for\nexample, \\(V\\) contained a number \\(c>v\\) then replacing it by the\nsmaller number \\(v\\) can only enlarge the set \\(\\{a\\mid a\\notin V\\}\\).\nHence we may assume\n\n\\[\nV=\\{1,2,\\dots ,v\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{1,2,\\dots ,h\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{2,3,\\dots ,d+1\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWith (5), condition (4) becomes\n\n\\[\na\\ge v+1,\\qquad b\\ge h+1,\\qquad a+b\\ge d+2 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(v+h+d=n-2\\),\n\n\\[\nd+2=n+1-(v+h). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsider the three lattice points\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(v+1,\\;h+1),\\qquad\nP_{2}=(v+2,\\;h+1),\\qquad\nP_{3}=(v+1,\\;h+2). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTheir sums are\n\n\\[\ns_{1}=v+h+2,\\qquad s_{2}=v+h+3,\\qquad s_{3}=v+h+3 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(s_{1}=v+h+2\\ge n+1-(v+h)=d+2\\); the same inequality\nholds for \\(s_{2},s_{3}\\) because \\(v+h+3\\ge v+h+2\\).  Moreover,\n\\(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\\le n+1\\) because \\(v+h\\le n-2\\).  Hence each of the\npoints \\(P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\) satisfies the three inequalities in (6) and\ntherefore is uncovered by the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nThus at least three points of \\(T_n\\) remain uncovered, but only two\nsunny lines are available – a contradiction.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq2\\text{ for any }n\\ge3}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence the\npoint lies on the line \\(x+y=a+b\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, so this\ngives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1 \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1 . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore\n\\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  Every point of \\(T_n\\) either has abscissa \\(\\le n-1\\)\n(and lies on one of the vertical lines) or is the point \\((n,1)\\) (and\nlies on \\(\\ell\\)).  Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly one\nsunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n#### 4.1  Sunny lines covering the “small‑sum’’ part\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y=-\\,i\\,x+i(k+2). \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-i\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each\n\\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(a\\le k\\).  Substituting \\(b=k+2-a\\) into the\nequation of \\(L_a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=-a\\cdot a+a(k+2)=k+2-a,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  Hence **every** lattice point\nof \\(T_n\\) whose sum does not exceed \\(k+1\\) belongs to one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n#### 4.2  Diagonal (non‑sunny) lines covering the “large‑sum’’ part\n\nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are those with sum  \n\n\\[\ns\\in\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-(k+1)=n-k\\) such values of \\(s\\).  Use the\n\\(n-k\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s\\qquad(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1). \\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines is non‑sunny, they are distinct, and together with\nthe \\(k\\) sunny lines from (14) we have precisely \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) lines.\n\n#### 4.3  Verification\n\n*If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\)*, then by the argument of\n§4.1 it lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\)*, then it lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\),\nwhich is one of the lines listed in (16).\n\nThus **all** points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.  The construction uses\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, so it works for\nevery integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final answer\n\nCombining the three parts we obtain:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) – possible (all diagonal lines).  \n* \\(k=1\\) – possible (all vertical lines plus one sunny line).  \n* \\(k=2\\) – impossible (Section 1).  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) – possible (Section 4).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎"
