) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines. Consequently the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; each of them is vertical, horizontal or diagonal.\u201d\n  - **Verification:** The premise is a legitimate starting point for a proof by contradiction.\n\n- **Quote:** \u201cLet  \n  \\[\n  V=\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n  H=\\{c\\mid y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\n  D=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n  \\]\n  and denote  \n  \\[\n  v=|V|,\\;h=|H\\|,\\;d=|D\\|,\\qquad v+h+d=n-2 .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\u201d\n  - **Verification:** Correct notation; the equality follows because the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines are partitioned into the three types.\n\n- **Quote:** \u201cA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is **uncovered** precisely when none of its three associated lines belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\); i.e.  \n  \\[\n  a\\notin V,\\qquad b\\notin H,\\qquad a+b\\notin D . \\tag{4}\n  \\]\u201d\n  - **Verification:** Correct; a point is covered iff at least one of the three lines containing it is present.\n\n#### 2.2 Reduction to initial\u2011segment sets  \n\n- **Quote:** \u201cReplacing each of the three sets by the initial segment of the same cardinality can only increase the set of uncovered points: if, for example, \\(V\\) contained a number \\(c>v\\) then replacing it by the smaller number \\(v\\) can only enlarge the set \\(\\{a\\mid a\\notin V\\}\\). Hence we may assume  \n  \\[\n  V=\\{1,2,\\dots ,v\\},\\qquad\n  H=\\{1,2,\\dots ,h\\},\\qquad\n  D=\\{2,3,\\dots ,d+1\\}. \\tag{5}\n  \\]\u201d\n  - **Issue:** **Justification Gap.**  \n    The argument that \u201creplacing by the initial segment can only increase the uncovered set\u2019\u2019 is intuitively plausible (the complement of a set grows when the set is moved towards smaller numbers), but a rigorous proof is omitted. One must show that for any fixed \\(v\\) the set \\(\\{a\\mid a\\notin V\\}\\) is indeed minimized when \\(V=\\{1,\\dots ,v\\}\\)