n uses exactly one sunny line. No errors.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Section\u202f4 \u2013 Construction for \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n#### 5.1 Definition of the sunny lines  \n\n- **Quote:** \u201cFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;y=-\\,i\\,x+i(k+2). \\tag{14}\n  \\]  \n  The slope \\(-i\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\u201d\n  - **Issue:** **Critical Error.**  \n    When \\(i=1\\) the slope is \\(-1\\), which is precisely the forbidden slope. Hence \\(L_1\\) is **not** sunny; it is the diagonal line \\(x+y=k+2\\). The claim that *all* \\(L_i\\) are sunny is false.\n\n#### 5.2 Claim that all \u201csmall\u2011sum\u2019\u2019 points are covered  \n\n- **Quote:** \u201cLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le k+1\\). \u2026 Substituting \\(b=k+2-a\\) into the equation of \\(L_a\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  b=-a\\cdot a+a(k+2)=k+2-a,\n  \\]  \n  so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\). Hence **every** lattice point of \\(T_n\\) whose sum does not exceed \\(k+1\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\u201d\n  - **Verification of covering:** The algebra is correct; for each \\(a\\) the point \\((a,k+2-a)\\) indeed satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\). However, for \\(a=1\\) the line used is \\(L_1\\), which is *non\u2011sunny*. Thus the covering property holds, but the statement that **all** these covering lines are sunny is false.\n\n#### 5.3 Non\u2011sunny diagonal lines for the \u201clarge\u2011sum\u2019\u2019 part  \n\n- **Quote:** \u201cThe remaining points \u2026 have sum \\(s\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). Use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines  \n  \\[\n  x+y=s\\qquad(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1). \\tag{16}\n  \\]\u201d\n  - **Verification:** These are indeed non\u2011sunny lines and together with the \\(k\\) lines from (14) give a total of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) lines.\n\n#### 5.4 Claim about the number of sunny lines  \n\n- **Quote:** \u201cThus the construction uses exactly \\(k\