he points of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(s\\ge k+2\\) are exactly the points of the\n   diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=s,\\qquad s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   There are \\(n-(k+1)=n-k\\) such diagonals.  Use the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=s\\qquad(s=k+2,\\dots ,n+1). \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Each of these lines is non‑sunny, they are distinct, and together with\n   the \\(k\\) sunny lines from (12) we have exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct\n   lines.\n\n   *Step 5.3 – Verification.*  \n   – If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), then by Step 5.1 it lies on\n   \\(L_a\\).  \n   – If \\(a+b\\ge k+2\\), then it lies on the diagonal line\n   \\(x+y=a+b\\) which is one of the lines listed in (14).  \n\n   Hence **all** points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.  The construction uses\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, so it works for\n   every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   From Sections 2–5 we have:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable (explicit constructions).  \n   * \\(k=2\\) is impossible (Section 2).  \n   * For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists\n     (Section 5).\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are called *sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The value \\(k=2\\) is impossible\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all\npoints of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nConsequently the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them is\nvertical, horizontal or diagonal.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{c\\mid y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\nand denote  \n\n\\[\nv=|V|,\\;h=|H|,\\;d=|D|,\\qquad v+h+d=n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is **uncovered** precisely when none of its\nthree associated lines belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\na\\notin V,\\qquad b\\notin H,\\qquad a+b\\notin D . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nReplacing each of the three sets by the initial segment of the same\ncardinality can only increase the set of uncovered points: if, for\nexample, \\(V\\) contained a number \\(c>v\\) then replacing it by the\nsmaller number \\(v\\) can only enlarge the set \\(\\{a\\mid a\\notin V\\}\\).\nHence we may assume\n\n\\[\nV=\\{1,2,\\dots ,v\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{1,2,\\dots ,h\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{2,3,\\dots ,d+1\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWith (5), condition (4) becomes\n\n\\[\na\\ge v+1,\\qquad b\\ge h+1,\\qquad a+b\\ge d+2 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(v+h+d=n-2\\),\n\n\\[\nd+2=n+1-(v+h). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsider the three lattice points\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(v+1,\\;h+1),\\qquad\nP_{2}=(v+2,\\;h+1),\\qquad\nP_{3}=(v+1,\\;h+2). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nTheir sums are\n\n\\[\ns_{1}=v+h+2,\\qquad s_{2}=v+h+3,\\qquad s_{3}=v+h+3 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) we have \\(s_{1}=v+h+2\\ge n+1-(v+h)=d+2\\); the same inequality\nholds for \\(s_{2},s_{3}\\) because \\(v+h+3\\ge v+h+2\\).  Moreover,\n\\(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\\le n+1\\) because \\(v+h\\le n-2\\).  Hence each of the\npoints \\(P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\) satisfies the three inequalities in (6) and\ntherefore is uncovered by the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nThus at least three points of \\(T_n\\) remain uncovered, but only two\nsunny lines are available – a contradiction.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq2\\text{ for any }n\\ge3}. \\tag