\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ntogether with a sunny line through the only point of \\(T_n\\) not covered by (4), namely \\((n,1)\\).  \nFor instance  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=x-n+1\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and passes through \\((n,1)\\).  Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  The case \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nFor each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_a:\\;y=-(a+1)x+\\bigl(k+1+a^{2}\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*The slope* \\(-(a+1)\\) is never \\(0\\), never infinite, and never \\(-1\\); consequently every \\(L_a\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\n*Coverage of the “small‑sum’’ points.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) then \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(b=k+1-a\\).  Substituting these values into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=-(a+1)a+(k+1+a^{2})=k+1-a,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  Hence **all** points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum does not exceed \\(k+1\\) are covered by the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n*Coverage of the remaining points.*  \nThe points with sum \\(s\\ge k+2\\) are exactly those lying on the diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s,\\qquad s=k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-(k+1)=n-k\\) such diagonals; we use them as the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n\nTogether the \\(k\\) sunny lines (6) and the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (7) give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\).  Thus a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Impossibility for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nConsequently the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are non‑sunny; write  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nH=\\{c\\mid y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\n\\]\n\nand denote  \n\n\\[\nv=|V|,\\qquad h=|H|,\\qquad d=|D|,\\qquad v+h+d=n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is **uncovered** by the non‑sunny lines precisely when  \n\n\\[\na\\notin V,\\qquad b\\notin H,\\qquad a+b\\notin D .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\na_{0}=\\min\\bigl([1,n]\\setminus V\\bigr),\\qquad\nb_{0}=\\min\\bigl([1,n]\\setminus H\\bigr).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\([1,n]\\setminus H\\) has cardinality \\(n-h\\).  \nAmong those \\(b\\)’s, at most \\(d\\) give a sum belonging to \\(D\\).  Hence the number of \\(b\\) satisfying (9) with \\(a=a_{0}\\) is at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-h)-d = n-h-\\bigl(n-2-(v+h)\\bigr)=v+2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(v\\ge1\\)* then (11) yields at least three distinct uncovered points \\((a_{0},b)\\), contradicting the fact that only two sunny lines are available to cover uncovered points.  \nTherefore we must have \\(v=0\\).  By symmetry the same argument forces \\(h=0\\).\n\nThus **all** non‑sunny lines are diagonal; i.e. \\(d=n-2\\) and  \n\n\\[\nD\\subset\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |D|=n-2 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nExactly two sums, say \\(s_{1}<s_{2}\\), are missing from \\(D\\).  \nFor a fixed sum \\(s\\) the number of lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\) equals \\(s-1\\).  Consequently the points whose sums are \\(s_{1}\\) and \\(s_{2}\\) contribute at least  \n\n\\[\n(s_{1}-1)+(s_{2}-1)\\ge (2-1)+(3-1)=3\n\\]\n\nuncovered points.  Again we would need at least three sunny lines, a contradiction.\n\nHence **no** configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Existence for \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\)\n\n*\\(n=3\\).*  \nTake the non‑sunny line \\(x+y=2\\) (covers \\((1,1)\\)) and the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\;y=-2x+5\\quad\\text{through }(1,2),\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}:\\;y=-3x+6\\quad\\text{through }(2,1).\n\\]\n\nThese three distinct lines cover the whole set \\(T_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) and contain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n*\\(n=4\\).*  \nTake the non‑sunny line \\(x=1\\) (covers all points with abscissa 1) and the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:\\;y=-2x+6\\quad\\text{through }(2,2),\\qquad\n\\ell_{2}:\\;y=-3x+9\\quad\\text{through }(3,1).\n\\]\n\nTogether with the vertical line \\(x=1\\) they cover all points of  \n\n\\[\nT_{4}=\\{(1,b)\\;(b=1\\ldots 4),\\;(2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(4,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nagain using exactly two sunny lines.\n\nThus \\(k=2\\) is attainable for \\(n=3,4\\) but impossible for all larger \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final classification\n\nCollecting the results:\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are possible (constructions in §§ 1.1 and 1.2).\n* For \\(n=3,4\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is possible (explicit constructions in § 2.2).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a construction exists (§ 1.3).\n* For \\(n\\ge5\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible (§ 2.1).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}, & n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n∎"
