s (4) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Hence for **every** \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n7. **Collecting the possibilities.**  \n\n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are possible.  \n   * The value \\(k=2\\) is possible **only** for \\(n=3\\).  \n   * For every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists (construction in \u00a7\u202f6).  \n\n   Therefore the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{cases}\n   \\{0,1,2,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n   \\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{k\\mid 3\\le k\\le n\\}, & n\\ge4 .\n   \\end{cases}}\n   \\]\n\n   (For \\(n=4\\) this gives \\(\\{0,1,3,4\\}\\); for larger \\(n\\) all integers from \\(3\\) up to \\(n\\) are allowed.) \u220e"

